course: dictionary
lesson: 4011
title: Gargle
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To gargle means to hold liquid at the back of your throat and blow air through it so it bubbles. People do this to clean or soothe a sore throat. The phrase «gargle with salt water» tells you the liquid is often salty and warm.
explain: The French r example gives a clue: «The French r is made at the back of the mouth, like a soft gargle: rouge (red) sounds like "ROOZH".» Here a gargle is a soft, bubbly sound made at the back of the throat. Try your meaning in the sentence: "like a soft bubbling at the back of the throat" still makes sense.
explain: The second example adds a clue: «The French r is made at the back of the throat, like a soft gargle, not with the tip of the tongue.» Both sentences point to the same place, the back of the throat. When you learn a word, check the other sentences too, because they often give more clues.
fact: Gargle (verb) means to hold liquid in the back of your throat and blow air through it to make it bubble.
fact: A gargle (noun) is the act of gargling, or the liquid you use for it, such as salt water.
fact: «Gargle with salt water» is a common home remedy for a sore throat.
fact: People often gargle mouthwash after brushing their teeth.
fact: Gargle can also describe a bubbling sound, like the soft gargle of a French r.
exercise: «Gargle with salt water when your throat is sore.» What does gargle mean? | To sing loudly in the bath | To wash your hair with shampoo | *To hold liquid in your throat and blow air through it to make it bubble | To eat a big meal very quickly
exercise: «The French r is made at the back of the mouth, like a soft gargle.» What does soft gargle suggest about the sound? | A loud sound made with the tip of the tongue | *A soft, bubbly sound made at the back of the throat | A sharp click made with the lips | A high whistle
exercise: Which best describes gargle as a noun? | *The act of gargling, or the liquid used for it | A loud shout at a football match | A sore throat | A type of sweet
test: Why might someone gargle after brushing their teeth? | To make their teeth grow | To warm up for a race | *To rinse their mouth with mouthwash | To go to sleep
test: Yesterday Sam ___ with warm salt water because his throat was sore. Which form fits? | *gargled | gargling | gargles | gargle
exam: In «Mia gargled with warm salt water», what does gargled mean? | Shouted loudly for help | Ate her lunch slowly | *Held salt water in her throat and blew air through it to make it bubble | Washed her hands with soap
